# ProjectAVR — configuration
#
# Copy to `.env` and fill in. Everything here has a working default except the
# identity provider, which deliberately does not: with no provider key the
# server falls back to a stub that approves every document and every face, and
# says so at startup and in the creator portal.
#
# Read by the npm scripts via `node --env-file-if-exists=.env`. Running
# `node server/index.js` by hand does not pick it up — use `npm start` or
# `npm run dev`, or pass the flag yourself.
#
# `npm test` deliberately does not load it. The tests set their own
# environment, and a developer's .env leaking into a test run makes the result
# depend on whose machine it ran on.

# ---------------------------------------------------------------- identity --

# Leave unset for the stub. Setting one provider's API key is enough to select
# it; AVR_IDENTITY_PROVIDER only forces the choice. Setting both keys is an
# error rather than a guess, and a name this server does not know is fatal —
# falling back to the stub on a typo would approve every document and every
# face while reporting itself as working.
# AVR_IDENTITY_PROVIDER=didit
# AVR_DIDIT_API_KEY=...

# Three workflows, created in the Didit console under Application → Workflows.
# They are not interchangeable — see docs/IDENTITY.md.
#
#   enrol     OCR + liveness + face match      (creators, once)
#   faceauth  liveness + face match, no OCR    (creators, each browser session)
#   age       OCR only, NO selfie              (every account, once)
#
# The age workflow must not capture a selfie. It is the one every viewer
# passes, and a workflow that quietly collects a face turns a document check
# for the whole user base into biometric collection for the whole user base.
# None of Didit's stock workflows qualifies: all of them bundle liveness and
# face match, so build it by hand with features [{"feature": "OCR"}] alone.
# Not AGE_ESTIMATION — that infers age from a face.
#
# Confirm what a workflow really runs by reading the `features` array of a
# session created against it, not the console toggles. Two face-related
# settings default to true and cannot be turned off through the v3 API; they
# are inert without a feature that captures a face. See docs/IDENTITY.md.
# AVR_DIDIT_ENROL_WORKFLOW_ID=
# AVR_DIDIT_FACEAUTH_WORKFLOW_ID=
# AVR_DIDIT_AGE_WORKFLOW_ID=

# Florida's anonymous route needs no fourth workflow, and deliberately so.
#
# Fla. Stat. § 501.1737 requires that an anonymous route be *offered* alongside
# the standard one and that the viewer choose between them, where anonymous
# means the check is run by an independent third party that does not retain
# identifying information. A workflow cannot be that whatever its retention
# schedule, because a workflow opens a session and a session exists afterwards.
# The server calls Didit's standalone Age Estimation endpoint instead, with
# `save_api_request: false` — nothing written, so nothing to delete. The API
# key above is the whole of its configuration, and where the estimate cannot
# run the route is not offered rather than silently swapped for the other one.
# See docs/STATE-AGE-LAWS.md.

# The age the estimate has to clear, which is deliberately not 18.
#
# The model's error is widest near the limit, and the limit is exactly where
# this check is used. Twenty-five spends that margin on the right side: an
# adult who reads young is declined and offered the document route, costing
# them a minute, where the reverse error costs a minor access. This is the
# "Challenge 25" convention from off-licence retail. Lower it and you are
# buying convenience with the thing the statute is about.
# AVR_AGE_ESTIMATION_MIN=25

# Didit resolves the face for a faceauth session from `vendor_data`, which
# carries our user id. Nothing is sent in `portrait_image`, so no face is ever
# held on this side — that is the property `db/schema.sql` is protecting, and
# it is the reason the two checks have to be the same vendor.
#
# Do not opt in to biometric template retention in the console. It keeps face
# embeddings after a session is deleted; the default, `delete_with_session`, is
# the one that matches what CONSENT_TEXT promises.

# Persona is still implemented and selectable, but is not the provider in use.
# It never got past two account-level blocks — server-side inquiry creation
# disabled, and a key with no inquiry-template read permission.
# AVR_PERSONA_API_KEY=persona_sandbox_...
# AVR_PERSONA_ENROL_TEMPLATE_ID=itmpl_...
# AVR_PERSONA_FACEAUTH_TEMPLATE_ID=itmpl_...
# AVR_PERSONA_AGE_TEMPLATE_ID=itmpl_...
# No anonymous template: Persona has no age-estimation endpoint, so on Persona
# the anonymous route is not offered at all rather than offered and faked.

# No webhook variable is listed here on purpose. `verifyPersonaSignature()`
# exists and is tested, but no webhook route is mounted, so a secret would
# configure nothing. Didit sessions are likewise polled, not pushed. See
# "Known gaps" in docs/IDENTITY.md.

# Refuse any broadcast that is not carrying a per-creator grant, and close
# the catalogue to accounts that have not passed an age check. The server
# refuses to start with this on and the stub selected.
# AVR_IDENTITY_ENFORCE=1

# How long an age check stands before it is asked for again (days).
# AVR_AGE_ASSURANCE_DAYS=730

# ---------------------------------------------------------------- where --
#
# Which of the two age gates a viewer gets is decided from where the request
# came from: a document check in the twenty-seven states with a statute, a
# single button everywhere else. The region is read per request, never stored
# on the account, because people travel — an Oregon button press must not
# carry into Texas. See server/identity/state-laws.js for the states and
# docs/STATE-AGE-LAWS.md for the design.
#
# The region comes from the edge, from whichever of these the deployment
# actually sets: cf-ipcountry/cf-region-code, x-vercel-ip-country(-region),
# cloudfront-viewer-country(-region), or x-geo-country/x-geo-region. Headers
# are only read when AVR_TRUST_PROXY matches the real hop count, otherwise a
# client can send its own.
#
# Cloudflare does NOT send cf-region-code by default. Without a Transform Rule
# adding it, every US viewer resolves to "US, state unknown" and falls through
# to AVR_GEO_FALLBACK below.

# What to do when the region cannot be worked out at all.
#
#   strict  treat them as covered and ask for a document   (default)
#   attest  treat them as uncovered and show the button
#
# The default is the expensive-but-safe one. Guessing "no statute" wrongly
# admits a Texan on a button press; guessing "covered" wrongly asks an Oregonian
# for a document they did not owe. Only one of those is a lawsuit.
# AVR_GEO_FALLBACK=strict

# Pin every request to one region, ignoring the edge entirely. For local work
# and for testing a particular state's gate — "US-FL" to see the Florida
# chooser, "US-TN" for the sixty-minute session, "US-OR" for the button.
# Never set this in production: it gives every viewer the same state.
# AVR_GEO_DEFAULT_REGION=US-TX

# What a viewer outside the United States gets.
#
#   attest  the button                                     (default)
#   verify  a document check
#
# No non-US regime is modelled here. The UK Online Safety Act, French Arcom
# rules and German JMStV all impose their own requirements that this code does
# not implement; `verify` is a blunt instrument for a deployment that would
# rather over-check than serve the button into one of them.
# AVR_AGE_NON_US=attest

# How long a button press stands before it is asked for again (days). Shorter
# than an assurance on purpose — it is worth much less, and re-asking is free.
# AVR_AGE_ATTESTATION_DAYS=365

# Upper bound on a portal unlock (seconds). Normally irrelevant — the token
# lives in sessionStorage and dies with the tab long before this.
# AVR_PORTAL_UNLOCK_TTL=7200

# How long a minted grant stays usable before the face check has to be redone
# (seconds). The window between pressing the button in the browser and the
# camera rolling — long enough to walk to the rig and scan the code, short
# enough that a screen left showing one is not a working credential later.
# AVR_GRANT_TTL=300

# How long after first use a grant may still be presented, to resume a
# broadcast that dropped (seconds). Measured from first use and never
# refreshed, so it bounds the total life of a spent grant. Zero means a lost
# Wi-Fi frame ends the broadcast and the creator walks back to a browser.
# AVR_GRANT_RESUME=90

# -------------------------------------------------------------------- auth --

# Required in production. Generate with:  openssl rand -base64 48
# AVR_JWT_SECRET=

# The account that runs this deployment, by email address. Promoted to
# `superuser` at boot; whoever held the role before is demoted to `admin` at the
# same moment, so clearing this variable actually clears the privilege. Set it
# before the account exists and the promotion happens at that signup instead.
#
# A superuser can reserve channel names and hold several names for one channel.
# Deliberately not grantable over HTTP: an endpoint that can hand out this role
# is an endpoint that can be made to hand out this role.
# AVR_SUPERUSER_EMAIL=

# ------------------------------------------------------------------- serve --

# PORT=8080
# AVR_MEDIA_DIR=./media
# AVR_DB=./data/avr.db

# ----------------------------------------------------------------- delivery --
#
# Packaging and what each headset gets offered. See docs/DELIVERY.md.

# Rungs per uploaded title. Each one is another full encode of the source, so
# this is the main lever on packaging cost.
# AVR_LADDER_RUNGS=5

# `h264,hevc` packages the ladder twice. HEVC carries the same picture at
# roughly 60% of the bitrate, and it is the only way an 8K rung reaches a
# standalone headset - a Quest 3 decodes 8K HEVC and does not decode 8K H.264.
# AVR_PACKAGE_CODECS=h264

# The brand mark burned into every rendition. If the file is missing the title
# is packaged unmarked rather than failing, and the catalog records that.
# AVR_WATERMARK=1
# AVR_WATERMARK_FILE=./art/logo-symbol.png
# AVR_WATERMARK_OPACITY=0.62

# Raise a decode ceiling this deployment has actually measured. These are
# judgement calls about other people's hardware; a self-hoster who has tested
# their own fleet knows better than the built-in table does.
# AVR_DEVICE_PROFILES={"quest2":{"maxHeight":4320,"maxBitrateKbps":80000}}

# A DLNA Browse request carries no User-Agent, so the ceiling is chosen rather
# than sniffed. SkyBox and Pigasus run on standalone headsets.
# AVR_DLNA_PROFILE=quest2

# --------------------------------------------------------------------- live --

# The shared contribution key, used when enforcement is off. It identifies
# nobody: every phone presents the same secret, so a session cannot be
# attributed to a creator. Generated at random each start if unset.
# AVR_LIVE_KEY=
# AVR_LIVE_PORT=8081

# TLS for the contribution socket. Both must be set or the listener stays
# cleartext and pairing links stop advertising TLS - a phone configured to
# expect it then fails closed with "the server refused an encrypted
# connection", which is the correct behaviour and a confusing one to debug.
# For a private address no CA will issue, generate a self-signed pair and let
# the pairing link carry its pin:
#
#   openssl req -x509 -newkey rsa:2048 -nodes -days 825 \
#     -keyout data/tls/ingest-key.pem -out data/tls/ingest-cert.pem \
#     -subj "/CN=ProjectAVR Live ingest" \
#     -addext "subjectAltName=IP:<lan-ip>,DNS:localhost"
#
# Regenerating the certificate changes the pin, so every saved destination on
# every phone must be paired again.
# AVR_LIVE_TLS_CERT=./data/tls/ingest-cert.pem
# AVR_LIVE_TLS_KEY=./data/tls/ingest-key.pem

# The host a pairing link tells the phone to connect to. Without it the link
# echoes back whatever hostname the browser used, so a creator page opened at
# `localhost:8080` mints a QR code pointing the phone at its own loopback.
# AVR_PUBLIC_INGEST_HOST=192.168.1.110

# ----------------------------------------------------------------- payments --

# `stub` is the only provider implemented, and it is the default. It takes no
# money: every purchase settles immediately and the credits are posted to the
# ledger as if they had been paid for. It says so on the console at startup and
# in the `provider` field of GET /api/credits, so nothing has to guess whether
# the money was real.
#
# An unrecognised name is fatal rather than a fall back to the stub, for the
# same reason the identity provider is: a typo that silently handed out free
# credits while reporting success is worse than a server that will not start.
#
# Credits are integer US cents, one credit to the cent. Dollars exist only in
# the purchase row and the button labels. See docs/MONETISATION.md.
# AVR_PAYMENTS_PROVIDER=stub

# How long a creator's streaming setup confirmation stands before it has to be
# redone (seconds, default 12 hours). It is also cleared on every sign-in,
# sign-out and sign-out-everywhere, and it is held in memory — a restart asks
# every creator to confirm again. This is a ceiling on a session that outlives
# a working rig, not the primary expiry.
# AVR_STREAM_CHECK_TTL=43200

# ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────── email ──
#
# `log` is the default and delivers nothing. It prints the recipient, the
# subject and the first line of every message to the console, so a signup mail
# can be seen firing without standing up a mail account, and it says on the
# console at startup that it is doing this.
#
# An unrecognised name is fatal rather than a fall back to `log`, for the same
# reason as the identity and payment providers: a typo that silently stopped
# delivering receipts and security notices while reporting every send as a
# success is worse than a server that will not start.
#
# Setting exactly one transport's key selects it; setting more than one is
# ambiguous and refused, and AVR_MAIL_PROVIDER resolves it.
# AVR_MAIL_PROVIDER=log
# AVR_RESEND_API_KEY=re_...
# AVR_POSTMARK_TOKEN=...

# The From address, and where replies go. Required by every real transport,
# which will reject an unverified sender — there is no plausible-looking
# default here, because a rejection that names the address is easier to fix
# than mail that silently goes nowhere.
# AVR_MAIL_FROM=LustXR <hello@example.com>
# AVR_MAIL_REPLY_TO=support@example.com

# The origin links in email point at, and the one origin trusted by CORS.
#
# Email is the one thing this server sends with no request to read its own
# address off, so unlike the DeoVR and playlist URLs this cannot be derived.
# Unset, links come out pointing at localhost.
#
# It is also the canonical entry in the CORS allowlist below. Unset, no origin
# may make a credentialed cross-origin request - which breaks nothing, because
# the app's own pages are same-origin and CORS does not apply to them.
# AVR_PUBLIC_URL=https://lustxr.example.com

# Other origins allowed to read credentialed responses, comma-separated.
#
# Only needed for a browser on a *different* origin that must read a response
# produced with a visitor's cookies - a second hostname, a staging front end.
# Native clients never send Origin and never enforce CORS, so DeoVR, Play'a,
# DLNA renderers and the capture app need nothing here. Public media, HLS and
# the DLNA endpoints are open to everyone without credentials regardless.
#
# Each entry is matched as an origin: scheme, host and port, no trailing path.
# AVR_CORS_ORIGINS=https://www.lustxr.example.com,https://staging.lustxr.example.com

# How long to wait on a mail transport's API for one message (ms).
# AVR_MAIL_TIMEOUT_MS=10000

# Postmark separates transactional and bulk traffic onto message streams, and
# sending marketing down the transactional one is how an account's sending
# reputation gets suspended. Only read when the transport is postmark.
# AVR_POSTMARK_STREAM=outbound
# AVR_POSTMARK_BROADCAST_STREAM=broadcast

# A mail server, rather than somebody's API.
#
# This is the transport this deployment actually runs on. Not by preference:
# Resend, Postmark, Mailgun, SendGrid and Mailchimp all prohibit this
# platform's content by name, so a hosted transport is not available at any
# price. docs/EMAIL.md records the reasoning.
#
# The host on its own is enough to select the transport.
# AVR_SMTP_HOST=mail.example.com

# 587 is submission and 465 is implicit TLS; the encryption mode follows from
# the port unless it is named. `none` is the only way to send in clear and has
# to be typed out — asked for starttls, the transport fails the send rather
# than continue unencrypted against a server that does not offer it.
# AVR_SMTP_PORT=587
# AVR_SMTP_TLS=starttls

# Omit both for a relay on loopback that authorises by source address. A user
# without a password is refused at startup rather than silently ignored.
# AVR_SMTP_USER=contact@example.com
# AVR_SMTP_PASS=

# The envelope sender, which is not always the From address. A submission
# server commonly binds the envelope to the login, while still allowing that
# login to send as one of its own aliases — which is how AVR_MAIL_FROM can be
# hello@ while the account authenticating is contact@. Defaults to the address
# out of AVR_MAIL_FROM.
# AVR_SMTP_ENVELOPE_FROM=contact@example.com

# What to announce in EHLO. Some servers check that it resolves, and none of
# them want to hear a container's hostname. Defaults to the envelope domain.
# AVR_SMTP_EHLO_NAME=mail.example.com


# -------------------------------------------------------- star biographies --
#
# The Top Stars row on the front page is an AEBN feed and needs nothing here.
# This is only for the hover card: a short, AI-written career profile of the
# performer, generated once per star and then cached to disk forever.
#
# Unset is a supported state. Without a key the row still renders and the
# hover card simply does not appear, which is what every deployment that has
# not bought into the feature should look like.
#
# A key here is a billable credential on a public endpoint. It is read by the
# server and never reaches the browser — see server/routes/stars.js for why
# that is not negotiable and what bounds the spend. Rotate it if it is ever
# pasted anywhere, including into a chat window.
# AVR_OPENAI_KEY=sk-proj-...

# Defaults to gpt-4o. These are profiles of real people, so the cost of a
# cheaper model is paid in invented award wins rather than in dollars — the
# whole row is ten calls, once, so there is very little to save.
# AVR_OPENAI_MODEL=gpt-4o

# AVR_OPENAI_TIMEOUT_MS=20000

# Where the generated profiles are kept. Defaults to data/star-bios.json.
# Deleting it re-buys every profile on the next hover; that is the only
# supported way to force a rewrite after changing the prompt or the model.
# AVR_STAR_BIO_CACHE=

# --- the live cam row ----------------------------------------------------
#
# Chaturbate's affiliate "white label": their online-rooms feed, rendered as a
# VR-first shelf on the front page, with the embedded player opening in-page.
# See server/routes/cams.js and docs/LIVE.md is *not* about this — this is an
# affiliate feed, not our own ingest.
#
# Unset is a supported and expected state. Without a code /api/cams answers
# {configured: false} and the shelf draws nothing at all. An invalid code is
# worse than no code and looks identical from the outside: Chaturbate answer
# {"results": []} to a wrong `wm` rather than an error, so a typo here reads
# as a quiet night rather than as a fault.
#
# This is the only thing tying a conversion to this site. It is read by the
# server and never reaches the browser — putting it in front-end code would
# put it in view-source and make swapping it for somebody else's a one-line
# user script.
# AVR_CHATURBATE_WM=

# How long one upstream fetch is shared by every visitor. Defaults to 45s.
# Much longer and the shelf shows thumbnails of rooms that have gone off air.
# AVR_CHATURBATE_TTL_MS=45000

# AVR_CHATURBATE_TIMEOUT_MS=8000
